# RE-RECORDED 2026-09-18 by #63's history purge, which is the ONE thing this file exists to make
# impossible to do silently. The purge replaced every RFC 1918 address in the tree, and nine recorded
# captures carried the lab's own bridge address in `diagnostics[0].url`
# (`http://<bridge>:5052/...` -> `http://REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:5052/...`).
#
# PASTING A DIGEST HERE ASSERTS THE CAPTURE IS STILL WHAT IT CLAIMS TO BE, so it was checked rather
# than assumed: each of the nine was diffed against its pre-purge blob (`00abaaab`) with the address
# substitution applied, and every one came back with ZERO other differences. No capture was re-taken;
# no environment, timestamp or finding moved. The recorded URL is redacted and nothing else.
#
# THE RECORDED PROVENANCE OF THE EVAL FIXTURES -- where each capture came from and what produced it.
#
# One SHA-256 per fixture, over that file's `url`/`capturedAt`/`baseUrl`/`cacheKey` values and its
# whole `environment` block, joined in document order. `recorded-provenance.test.ts` recomputes these
# and refuses any that has moved; that file's header carries the incident and the reasoning.
#
# A hex digest carries no product name, so no rename sweep can edit it into agreement -- which is the
# whole point, after `e435ac17` rewrote ten recorded capture URLs and every gate stayed green.
#
# CHANGING A LINE HERE ASSERTS that the new values describe where the evidence actually came from.
# It is not the way to quieten a failing test. If nothing was re-captured, restore the values.
#
# NOT `shasum -a 256 <file>` -- these hash the extracted provenance fields, not the file.
